# 10. Capability Expansion Strategy

Future capabilities must NOT be added as random conditions.

Wrong:

```
if coupon then discount
if bundle then price change
if sale then show badge
```

This creates the same problems Cartique is designed to solve.

Instead:

Every commercial capability must become a Kernel capability.

Example future structure:

```
src/

capabilities/

pricing/
    SalePriceCapability.js
    BulkPricingCapability.js

variants/
    VariantCapability.js

bundles/
    BundleCapability.js

coupons/
    CouponCapability.js

wishlist/
    WishlistCapability.js
```

Capabilities register with the Kernel.

The engine executes them.

---

# 11. Recommended Capability Development Order

## Phase 1 — Product Variants (First Priority)

This is foundational.

Example:

Chia Seeds:

```
100g  R100

200g  R160

500g  R230
```

Currently products are too flat.

Variants must become first-class commercial entities.

Required:

```
Product

↓

Variant

↓

Sellable Item

↓

Cart Line
```

The cart must store:

```
Product ID

Variant ID

Quantity

Resolved Price
```

This capability unlocks:

* clothing sizes
* colours
* weights
* subscription options
* packaging

---

## Phase 2 — Sale Pricing

Example:

Normal:

```
R100
```

Sale:

```
R80
```

Decision:

```
originalPrice:100

sellingPrice:80

adjustment:
sale_discount
```

Storefront displays:

```
R100 crossed out

R80

SALE badge
```

---

## Phase 3 — Coupons

Coupons belong in the Kernel.

Example:

Customer enters:

```
WELCOME10
```

The storefront passes:

```
Coupon Code

↓

Kernel

↓

Eligibility

↓

Adjustment

↓

Commercial Decision
```

The coupon should never directly modify cart totals.

---

## Phase 4 — Bundles

Example:

Bundle:

```
Shampoo
+
Conditioner
+
Hair Oil
```

The Kernel decides:

```
Individual total:
R300

Bundle price:
R250
```

The Storefront only displays the decision.

---

## Phase 5 — Wishlist

Wishlist is less commercial.

It belongs mostly in:

```
storefront/services
```

but should integrate with:

* customer identity
* product availability
* variants

